# What is NOT sent to the daemon as build context.
#
# This file now DECIDES what ends up in the image, which it did not before. The
# Dockerfile used to copy five named things (package*.json, *.js, contexts,
# protos, env) and no rule here touched any of them; since the 2026-08-23
# reorganisation put every module in a subdirectory it copies the context whole,
# so that adding a protocol directory cannot be forgotten. Every exclusion below
# is therefore a decision about the image as well as about upload time — read the
# Dockerfile's COPY comment before adding one.
#
# node_modules is the whole reason this file exists. It is tens of megabytes, it
# is re-sent on every build, and it is usually PRESENT: `npm ci` here is the
# normal way to run the service outside a container, and the project this was
# extracted from installs these dependencies in its checkout of this repository
# as well — its compose files build with `context: .` pointed at that checkout,
# and four of its host-run tests load bbs2023.js in place, so they need the
# dependencies on disk. Without this file every one of those builds pays for it.
**/node_modules

# The checkout's own history. When this repository is consumed as a git
# submodule its .git is a small FILE rather than a directory; either way it is
# not something a build needs.
.git
.gitignore

# The runtime artefacts .gitignore already keeps out of commits: the signing keys
# are regenerated on every start by design, so a stray copy is stale as well as
# private.
*.pem
*.log

# The documentation and the CI definitions. Neither is read at runtime: docs/ is
# the source of the GitHub Pages site, .github/ is the workflows that build it
# and the container, and the CLAUDE.md files are guidance for whoever (or
# whatever) edits this repository. They are excluded because the Dockerfile now
# copies the context whole — before that they were left out by not being named.
# `docs` WAS HERE until 2026-09-14 and went the way of the three files below:
# tests/error_codes.js asserts that docs/error-codes.md is what the error-code
# table generates and that docs/_config.yml lists it, so the directory is the
# subject of a test that runs in the TESTS image. The service Dockerfile
# removes it.
.github
# ...EXCEPT THE ONE WORKFLOW THAT IS THE SUBJECT OF A TEST (2026-09-10). This is
# the block below's argument a fourth step out: tests/teardown_bounds.js reads
# `./docker-run-tests.sh`'s own two wall clocks and this job's `timeout-minutes`
# and asserts the second is above the sum of the first — which is a claim about
# two files, one of which is a CI definition. `pages.yml` and
# `build-container.yml` stay out, because nothing asserts anything about them.
!.github/workflows/tests.yml
CLAUDE.md
**/CLAUDE.md
COVERAGE.md
SECURITY.md
LICENSE.md
clean.sh

# Claude Code's per-checkout state, and above all its AGENT WORKTREES: a
# session that isolates a subagent checks a second copy of this repository out
# under .claude/worktrees/, and the context is copied whole. On 2026-09-16 one
# appeared between two modes of ./docker-run-tests.sh and the next image
# carried it, so every test that walks the tree for source (error_codes,
# admin_actions_layer) found a second service in it and failed naming files
# that are not this one. .git/info/exclude keeps it out of `git status`, which
# is why nothing else noticed.
.claude

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE OF THEM CAME BACK ON 2026-09-09, AND THE REASON IS THE SAME ONE THE
# BLOCK BELOW GIVES ABOUT `tests`: THIS FILE SERVES TWO IMAGES THAT WANT
# OPPOSITE THINGS.
#
# `README.md`, `Dockerfile` and `docker-compose.yml` are still not read at
# runtime and are still not wanted in the service image. But they ARE the
# subject of three in-process jobs — tests/readme_ports.js reads the README's
# ports table and this image's own EXPOSE lines, and tests/postgres_schema.js
# reads the application role out of docker-compose.yml and checks it against
# postgres/schema.sql — and those jobs run in the TESTS image, where an absent
# file is not a skip but `ENOENT` reported as a failing test.
#
# THAT IS EXACTLY WHAT HAPPENED: both jobs were written on 2026-09-09, both
# passed on a checkout under `npm test`, and both failed on the first
# containerized run with an ENOENT naming a file that is plainly in the
# repository. A test whose subject is a file at the package root cannot see it
# unless the context carries it.
#
# The service image is unchanged: its Dockerfile removes all three in the same
# `rm` it already removes `tests` and `xacml-pep` with, where the reason sits
# beside the instruction.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `tests` IS NOT EXCLUDED HERE, AND IT WAS UNTIL 2026-08-29. THIS FILE IS SHARED
# BY TWO IMAGES NOW AND THEY WANT OPPOSITE THINGS FROM IT.
#
# The service image must not carry the suite; the TESTS image
# (tests/Dockerfile, built by docker-compose-run-tests.yml) is nothing but the
# suite. Both are built from THIS context — the tests image needs the whole
# source tree, because ten of its jobs require the service's own modules — and
# a build context has exactly one ignore file.
#
# BuildKit would allow two: it reads <dockerfile>.dockerignore in preference to
# this one. That was the first arrangement here and it lasted one build. The
# LEGACY builder ignores such a file completely, and a machine with no `docker
# buildx` plugin — docker 29 with the plugin not installed, which is an
# ordinary machine and was the one this was written on — silently uses it. The
# failure is `COPY failed: no source files were specified` on a COPY of a
# directory that is right there in the checkout, which names neither the ignore
# file nor the builder.
#
# So there is one rule set, `tests/` is in the context, and the SERVICE image
# removes the directory itself in one line of its Dockerfile — where the reason
# is next to the instruction rather than three files away. What that costs is
# the one thing the old comment here claimed: editing a test now busts the
# service image's final `COPY . ./` layer. That layer is a copy and an `rm`,
# below both `npm install`s, so it is seconds — and it buys a test suite that
# builds on every builder.
#
# The `test` script in package.json still does not work inside the service
# image, deliberately, because that image has no tests directory by the time it
# is finished.
# `docker-compose.yml` and `Dockerfile` ARE NOT HERE ANY MORE — see the block
# above; the service image removes them instead. Nor is
# `docker-compose-run-tests.yml`, since 2026-09-14, for the same reason:
# tests/stack_network.js and tests/teardown_bounds.js read it in the tests
# image.
.dockerignore

# The test REPORT, which `tests` no longer covers. It is generated, timestamped
# and gitignored, and a previous run's answer has no business being baked into
# either image — least of all the tests image, where the container mounts the
# host's directory over the top of it.
tests/report

# THE BUILD STAMP FROM A HOST RUN. `common/version.js --stamp .` writes
# version.json at the package root, and the Dockerfile runs it INSIDE the image
# after the source is copied — so a copy of the host's is at best redundant and
# at worst a lie: it would be the build number of whatever a developer stamped
# on their laptop, baked into the tests image (which does not stamp) and sitting
# in `docker history` for the service image. Anchored, so it names the package
# root's file only.
/version.json

# The persistent store — see .gitignore. A local `data/` directory from a host
# run must not be baked into the image: the container mounts a VOLUME at
# /usr/src/sts/data, and a copy inside the image would be shadowed by it on
# every run but would still be there in `docker history` with whatever was in
# the directory the day the image was built.
data/

# THE EMBEDDED DEBUGGER'S BUILT TREE, for the reason .gitignore gives: a local
# build of the other project. The image takes it from that project's embedded
# image (the Dockerfile's `debugger` stage), so a stale checkout copy must not
# reach the build context and shadow it.
debugger/embedded/

# TERRAFORM'S PROVIDER CACHE, from `terraform init` in deploy/aws/foundation and
# deploy/aws/environment: about 800 MB each, none of it read by either image,
# and on 2026-09-15 it made a 1.8 GB build context that the machine building
# the suite runner ran out of memory sending. The lock files beside them are
# committed and small, and neither image needs them either.
**/.terraform
